.TH STAY 1 "August 18, 2026" "stay 0.0.89" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
stay \- persistent tmux sessions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B stay
.RB [ --no-alt-screen ]
.br
.B stay
.RB [ --help | -h | --version | -V ]
.br
.B stay --prompt-integration
.br
.B stay list
.RB [ --json ]
.br
.B stay create
.I SESSION
.RI [ COMMAND ... ]
.RB [ -c\~DIR ]
.RB [ -f ]
.RB [ -a ]
.RB [ -r ]
.RB [ -L ]
.br
.B stay attach
.I SESSION
.RB [ -l\~FILE ]
.RB [ -t ]
.RB [ --raw ]
.RB [ -r ]
.RB [ -L ]
.RB [ -p ]
.br
.B stay kill
.I SESSION
.br
.B stay shell-integration
.RB [ --s-alias ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B stay
keeps terminal work in named tmux sessions.  Running it without a subcommand
opens an interactive picker.  The explicit commands are intended for scripts
and for cases where the session name is already known.
.PP
Stay uses the tmux server named
.B stay
and requires tmux 3.6 or newer.  It must be run from a terminal outside an
existing tmux client, except for the shell-integration commands and
.B --prompt-integration.
.PP
The picker remembers the last successfully attached session.  When the picker
returns after a detach, that session is selected so that pressing Enter again
continues the same workflow.  Attach errors are displayed when the picker
reopens.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B --prompt-integration
Print a POSIX shell prompt helper and exit.  The helper prints
.B [SESSION]
when
.B STAY_SESSION_NAME
is set in a Stay-created pane.  Enable the prompt segment with:
.PP
.EX
eval "$(stay --prompt-integration)"
.EE
.PP
Reference
.B stay_prompt_segment
from the shell prompt.  In zsh, also enable
.B PROMPT_SUBST.
This option is mutually exclusive with every subcommand and with every other
flag.
.TP
.B --no-alt-screen
Force the picker to draw on the terminal's main screen instead of using the
alternate screen.  This option is valid only when no subcommand is given.
Without it, the picker uses the alternate screen only when the terminal probe
shows that it is supported.
.TP
.B -h, --help
Print command usage and exit.  Help is available for the top-level command
and each public subcommand.
.TP
.B -V, --version
Print the installed Stay version and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Picker
With no subcommand, Stay opens the picker.  Use the arrow keys or Page Up and
Page Down to select a session.  Enter attaches to the selected session.  If
there is no selected session, Enter opens the create prompt.  The picker also
offers a synthetic create row when the inventory is empty.
.PP
The picker keys are:
.TP
.B Up, Down, Home, End, Page Up, Page Down
Move the selection.
.TP
.B v
Toggle view-only (read-only) attach for the selected session.
.TP
.B l
Toggle low-priority attach for the selected session.
.TP
.B /
Enter ordered fuzzy filter mode.  Filtering is case-insensitive.  Longer
queries tolerate small spelling errors and skipped candidate characters for
abbreviations; for example, \fBrealese\fR can find \fBrelease\fR, and \fBstwt\fR
can find \fBstaywtc\fR.  One- and two-character queries also use ordered
matching but do not allow spelling errors.  Enter attaches to the selected
match and Escape cancels the filter.
.TP
.B c
Open the create prompt.
.TP
.B Enter
Attach to the selected session, or create a session when no session is
selected.
.TP
.B r
Recreate the selected terminated session, or recreate the selected live
session directly after confirmation.
.TP
.B e
Edit the selected session name.
.TP
.B k
Kill the selected session after confirmation.
.TP
.B K
Kill all terminated sessions after confirmation.
.TP
.B q, Escape
Quit the picker.
.PP
The picker displays the live, detached, and terminated session inventory.  In
filter mode, an empty result is not actionable; Escape cancels and Enter
reports that there is no matching session.
.SS list
.TP
.B stay list
Print the human-readable session inventory.  When stdout is a terminal, the
inventory includes the interactive display form; otherwise it is suitable for
redirecting to a file.
.TP
.B stay list --json
Print a stable JSON inventory.  Session timestamps are RFC 3339 UTC values
with a trailing
.B Z.
.SS create
.TP
.BI "stay create " SESSION " [COMMAND ...]"
Create a named session.  If no command is supplied, use
.B STAY_CMD
when configured, or the shell from
.B SHELL
(falling back to
.B /bin/sh
).
.TP
.BI "-c, --cwd " DIR
Start the session in
.I DIR
instead of the caller's current directory.
.TP
.B -f, --force-recreate
Kill an existing session with this name and create it again.
.TP
.B -a, --attach
Attach immediately after creating the session.
.TP
.B -r, --read-only
With
.B --attach,
attach in view-only mode.  This option requires
.B --attach.
.TP
.B -L, --low-priority
With
.B --attach,
attach with low-priority terminal size behavior.  This option requires
.B --attach.
.PP
The session name must be a valid Stay session name.  When a command is given,
its first word must resolve to an executable on
.B PATH
or be an executable path.
.SS attach
.TP
.BI "stay attach " SESSION
Attach to an existing session.  Detach using the configured detach control
key, normally Ctrl+\\.
.TP
.B -r, --read-only
Attach in view-only mode.
.TP
.B -L, --low-priority
Attach with low-priority terminal size behavior.
.TP
.BI "-l, --log " FILE
Capture session output in
.I FILE
while attached.  Relative paths are resolved against the caller's current
directory.  Existing log files must be regular, owner-only files owned by the
current user; symlinks and group/other permissions are refused.  New logs are
created owner-only.
.TP
.B -t, --truncate
Replace the existing log contents before capturing.  This option requires
.B --log.
.TP
.B --raw
Capture ANSI escape sequences through a continuous tmux pipe.  This option
requires
.B --log.
.TP
.B -p, --pass-through
Forward stdin to the session without opening an interactive attach.  The
command exits after stdin reaches end of file.  Pass-through cannot be
combined with
.B --read-only,
.B --low-priority,
or
.B --log
(and therefore cannot be combined with
.B --truncate
or
.B --raw
either).
.PP
Without
.B --raw,
logging captures clean output incrementally from retained tmux history and
captures the visible screen at the final detach boundary.  Reattaching appends
only output not already present in the log.  With
.B --raw,
ANSI sequences are retained and output continues through the tmux pipe while
the session is detached.
.SS kill
.TP
.BI "stay kill " SESSION
Kill an existing session.  Killing the last session may stop the Stay tmux
server; a subsequent empty listing is normal.
.SS shell-integration
.TP
.B stay shell-integration
Print a POSIX shell snippet that defines
.B stay_prompt_segment.
Source it from a shell startup file, for example:
.PP
.EX
eval "$(stay shell-integration)"
.EE
.TP
.B --s-alias
Also print
.B alias s=stay
when no existing alias, executable, or unreadable startup file conflicts with
the short name.  A conflict is reported as a warning and the alias is omitted.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Stay reads
.B stay/config.toml
below the platform user configuration directory.  This is normally
.B ~/.config/stay/config.toml
on Linux and
.B ~/Library/Application Support/stay/config.toml
on macOS.  The standard platform configuration rules apply, including
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
on systems that provide it.
.PP
The supported TOML keys are:
.TP
.B default_command
Command string used when a session is created without an explicit command.
.TP
.B detach_key
Control key that detaches from a session.  The default is
.B Ctrl+\e.
.TP
.B copy_mode_key
Control key that enters tmux copy mode.  The default is
.B Ctrl+Space.
.TP
.B history_lines
Number of tmux history lines to retain, or the string
.B unlimited
(implemented as a large bounded history).  The default is 10,000.
.TP
.B log_capture_interval_seconds
Positive integer number of seconds between clean-mode log captures.  The
default is 5.
.PP
Environment variables override the corresponding configuration keys:
.B STAY_CMD,
.B STAY_DETACH_KEY,
.B STAY_COPY_MODE_KEY,
.B STAY_HISTORY_LINES,
and
.B STAY_LOG_CAPTURE_INTERVAL_SECONDS.
Key values use names such as
.B Ctrl+X,
.B Ctrl+Space,
and
.B Ctrl+[.
The detach and copy-mode keys must resolve to different control bytes.
.PP
Stay sets
.B STAY_SESSION_NAME
inside each Stay-created session for prompt integration.  It is runtime
session metadata, not a configuration override.
.SH EXIT STATUS AND ERRORS
.TP
.B 0
Stay completed successfully, or an attached pane exited with status zero.
Help and version output also exit zero.
.TP
.B 1
A runtime error occurred, such as missing tmux, an unsupported tmux version,
an absent session, an invalid configuration, or an attach/logging failure.
An attached pane may also legitimately return status 1.
.TP
.B 2
Command-line parsing or cross-option validation failed.  Usage errors are
written to standard error.
.TP
.B Attached pane status
For an explicit
.B stay attach
command, or
.B stay create --attach,
Stay returns the attached pane's exit status after it exits.  When the pane is
terminated by signal N, the status is 128+N.  Consequently, explicit attach
can return any status in the platform's 8-bit exit-status range; those
statuses are distinct from Stay's own runtime and parsing errors.
.PP
Stay requires tmux 3.6 or newer and reports a clear error when tmux is absent
or too old.  It reports errors from the interactive picker, session lifecycle,
configuration, and logging operations on standard error with the
.B stay:
prefix.
.SH HOMEBREW
The Homebrew tap installs the target-native Stay release archive and its
section-1 manual page.  After installing with:
.PP
.EX
brew tap nevdelap/stay
brew install nevdelap/stay/stay
.EE
.PP
read this page with:
.PP
.EX
man stay
.EE
